    <div class="hero">
        <div class="tagline">Measure. Analyze. Optimize. Profit.</div>
        <h1>ADR, RevPAR, GOPPAR Explained</h1>
        <p>
            Key Hotel Performance Metrics Every Hotelier &amp; Finance Professional Must Track
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
        <div class="article-card">

            <div class="author-strip">
                <img src="/assets/nigel-thomas-headshot.jpg" alt="Nigel Anthony Thomas, hospitality executive and corporate trainer">
                <div>
                    <div class="author-name">Nigel Anthony Thomas</div>
                    <div class="author-role">Hospitality Executive &amp; Corporate Trainer &bull; CHS, ServSafe Food Protection Manager &bull; 30+ Years Across Hotels, Resorts &amp; Cruise Lines</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>
                In three decades of hotel operations across India, the Middle East and the United States, I've seen too many properties fixate on one metric while ignoring the others. A hotel can have a great ADR and still bleed profit. It can have a high RevPAR and still miss the full picture of what's happening across the property. The real discipline is knowing which number to look at, when, and how they all fit together.
            </p>

            <p>
                This guide breaks down the essential hotel performance metrics — the ones I keep in front of every operations team I lead. If you're a hotelier, revenue manager, or finance professional, these are the numbers that tell you whether your property is healthy, where the leverage points are, and what to do next.
            </p>

            <div class="quote-banner">
                "A single metric tells you one thing. The relationship between them tells you everything."
            </div>

            <h2>1. Key Hotel Performance Metrics</h2>

            <p>
                These five metrics form the foundation of hotel performance measurement. Each one answers a different question about your business — from pricing power to operational efficiency.
            </p>

            <div class="metrics-table-wrap">
                <table class="metrics-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Metric</th>
                            <th>Full Form</th>
                            <th>Definition</th>
                            <th>Formula</th>
                            <th>Why It Matters</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="metric-name">ADR</td>
                            <td>Average Daily Rate</td>
                            <td>The average rate obtained for occupied rooms.</td>
                            <td><span class="formula">Total Room Revenue ÷ Rooms Sold</span></td>
                            <td>Indicates pricing performance and revenue potential.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="metric-name">Occupancy %</td>
                            <td>Occupancy Percentage</td>
                            <td>The percentage of rooms sold out of rooms available.</td>
                            <td><span class="formula">Rooms Sold ÷ Rooms Available × 100</span></td>
                            <td>Shows how well the hotel is filling its available inventory.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="metric-name">RevPAR</td>
                            <td>Revenue Per Available Room</td>
                            <td>The revenue generated per available room.</td>
                            <td><span class="formula">Total Room Revenue ÷ Rooms Available</span></td>
                            <td>Combines occupancy and rate to measure overall room revenue performance.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="metric-name">GOPPAR</td>
                            <td>Gross Operating Profit Per Available Room</td>
                            <td>The gross operating profit generated per available room.</td>
                            <td><span class="formula">Gross Operating Profit ÷ Rooms Available</span></td>
                            <td>Shows profitability efficiency of operations.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="metric-name">TREVPAR</td>
                            <td>Total Revenue Per Available Room</td>
                            <td>Total revenue generated from all departments per available room.</td>
                            <td><span class="formula">Total Revenue ÷ Rooms Available</span></td>
                            <td>Measures total revenue productivity across all departments.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p>
                Each metric serves a specific purpose. ADR tells you about your pricing power. Occupancy tells you about demand. RevPAR tells you about room revenue efficiency. GOPPAR tells you about profit. And TREVPAR tells you about total revenue productivity across the entire property.
            </p>

            <h2>2. How the Metrics Are Related</h2>

            <p>
                These metrics don't exist in isolation. They flow into each other in a clear sequence, and understanding that sequence is the key to diagnosing performance and taking action.
            </p>

            <div class="flow-wrapper">
                <div class="flow-row">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flow-box">ADR</div>
                        <span class="flow-label">Pricing</span>
                    </div>
                    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                    <div>
                        <div class="flow-box">Occupancy %</div>
                        <span class="flow-label">Volume</span>
                    </div>
                    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                    <div>
                        <div class="flow-box">RevPAR</div>
                        <span class="flow-label">Room Revenue</span>
                    </div>
                    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                    <div>
                        <div class="flow-box">Total Revenue</div>
                        <span class="flow-label flow-sub">Rooms + F&amp;B + Other</span>
                    </div>
                    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                    <div>
                        <div class="flow-box">GOPPAR</div>
                        <span class="flow-label">Profitability</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div style="margin-top:16px;color:#888;font-size:0.9rem;border-top:1px solid #222;padding-top:14px;">
                    Higher ADR and Occupancy lead to higher RevPAR, which ultimately drives GOPPAR and profitability.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Why this sequence matters</strong><br><br>
                You can't grow GOPPAR without understanding what's driving RevPAR. And you can't improve RevPAR without knowing whether you're moving the right lever — rate or volume. This flow gives you the map. The metrics tell you where you are on it.
            </div>

            <h2>3. Worked Example: 100-Room Hotel</h2>

            <p>
                Let's walk through a full example using a 100-room hotel over a 30-day period (3,000 room nights available). This is the kind of calculation I run with every property I work with — it's simple arithmetic, but it reveals where the profit really sits.
            </p>

            <div class="example-grid">
                <div class="example-card">
                    <h4>📊 Revenue &amp; Operating Data</h4>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">Rooms Available</span><span class="value">3,000 Room Nights</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">Rooms Sold</span><span class="value">2,100 Room Nights</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">Total Room Revenue</span><span class="value">₹115,700</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">F&amp;B Revenue</span><span class="value">₹7,000</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">Other Revenue (Laundry, Spa, Parking, etc.)</span><span class="value">₹1,250</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item" style="border-top:2px solid #333;margin-top:6px;padding-top:10px;">
                        <span class="label" style="font-weight:600;">Total Revenue</span>
                        <span class="value total">₹124,000</span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="example-card">
                    <h4>📉 Expenses &amp; Profit</h4>
                    <div class="line-item"><span class="label">Departmental Expenses (Rooms + F&amp;B + Others)</span><span class="value">₹115,000</span></div>
                    <div class="line-item" style="border-top:2px solid #333;margin-top:6px;padding-top:10px;">
                        <span class="label" style="font-weight:600;">Gross Operating Profit</span>
                        <span class="value total">₹9,000</span>
                    </div>
                    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85rem;color:#999;border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;padding-top:12px;">
                        <em>Departmental expenses include all direct operating costs for Rooms, F&amp;B and other revenue centres.</em>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 style="margin-top:10px;">Metric Calculations</h3>

            <p>
                Using the formulas from the table above, here's how each metric is calculated from the example data:
            </p>

            <div class="result-grid">
                <div class="result-chip">
                    <span class="num">₹55.10</span>
                    <span class="label">ADR</span>
                    <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#777;display:block;margin-top:3px;">115,700 ÷ 2,100</span>
                </div>
                <div class="result-chip">
                    <span class="num">70%</span>
                    <span class="label">Occupancy</span>
                    <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#777;display:block;margin-top:3px;">2,100 ÷ 3,000 × 100</span>
                </div>
                <div class="result-chip">
                    <span class="num">₹38.57</span>
                    <span class="label">RevPAR</span>
                    <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#777;display:block;margin-top:3px;">115,700 ÷ 3,000</span>
                </div>
                <div class="result-chip">
                    <span class="num">₹3.00</span>
                    <span class="label">GOPPAR</span>
                    <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#777;display:block;margin-top:3px;">9,000 ÷ 3,000</span>
                </div>
                <div class="result-chip">
                    <span class="num">₹41.33</span>
                    <span class="label">TREVPAR</span>
                    <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#777;display:block;margin-top:3px;">124,000 ÷ 3,000</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>
                Notice that RevPAR (₹38.57) is not the same as ADR (₹55.10). The difference is occupancy. If this property could increase occupancy from 70% to 80% without dropping ADR, RevPAR would jump to ₹44.08 — a 14% improvement. That's the leverage point.
            </p>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>From the floor</strong><br><br>
                I've worked with hotels where the team chased ADR aggressively and lost occupancy, only to watch RevPAR fall. I've also worked with properties that dropped rate to fill rooms and saw RevPAR improve — but GOPPAR didn't follow because the added volume came at a cost. The relationship between these numbers is always more revealing than any single one.
            </div>

            <h2>4. Why These Metrics Matter in Practice</h2>

            <p>
                In day-to-day hotel operations, these metrics serve different purposes at different levels of the organization:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>ADR</strong> — The revenue manager's primary lever. It tells you whether your pricing strategy is working relative to your comp set.</li>
                <li><strong>Occupancy</strong> — The sales team's scorecard. It tells you whether demand generation is keeping pace with supply.</li>
                <li><strong>RevPAR</strong> — The general manager's dashboard. It tells you whether your room revenue engine is firing on all cylinders.</li>
                <li><strong>GOPPAR</strong> — The owner's bottom line. It tells you whether the property is actually profitable after all the noise.</li>
                <li><strong>TREVPAR</strong> — The operator's full picture. It tells you whether you're maximizing revenue across every department.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="quote-banner">
                "RevPAR tells you how well you're selling rooms. GOPPAR tells you how well you're running a business."
            </div>

            <h2>5. Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>

            <p>
                In my experience, the most common mistakes hoteliers make with these metrics are:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>Chasing ADR at the expense of occupancy</strong> — A higher rate with fewer rooms sold often lowers RevPAR and GOPPAR.</li>
                <li><strong>Filling rooms at any cost</strong> — Deep discounting can boost occupancy but destroy GOPPAR if the marginal cost of each room exceeds the marginal revenue.</li>
                <li><strong>Ignoring TREVPAR</strong> — Room revenue is important, but total revenue per available room tells you whether your F&amp;B, spa and other departments are pulling their weight.</li>
                <li><strong>Looking at metrics in isolation</strong> — A high RevPAR with a low GOPPAR means you're spending too much to generate that revenue. The relationship is where the diagnosis lives.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

            <p>
                These five metrics — ADR, Occupancy, RevPAR, GOPPAR and TREVPAR — form the backbone of hotel performance measurement. None of them is perfect on its own. Together, they tell a complete story about pricing, demand, revenue efficiency, profitability and total productivity.
            </p>

            <p>
                The properties that consistently outperform their comp set aren't the ones that obsess over a single number. They're the ones that understand how the numbers fit together, track them weekly (not just monthly), and act on what they reveal — adjusting pricing, managing costs, and optimizing the mix across departments.
            </p>

            <div class="quote-banner">
                "Numbers don't lie — but they do tell different stories depending on which one you're looking at. Learn to read them all."
